this Christmas shoppers are set to spend  billions of pounds on toys in the UK but  with some of the latest must-have toy  tech gadgets come serious security  concerns the consumer group which has  found a number of smart toys could leave  children at risk of being contacted by  strangers  we tested 7 children's connected toys  that are sold across major retailers in  the UK such as John Lewis Amazon Argos  and Smith's and found that three of them  had security flaws that would enable  someone else to connect to the toy and  potentially communicate with the child  playing with it  smart toys in the UK are being marketed  to children as youngest three years old  but how do those buying toys this  Christmas feel about this potential  security failure yes quite worrying it's  a thing you've got to think about I mean  nowadays as technology's changing  there's lots of barriers and things  they've put up so we'll see see how code  as it goes up for now we know we might  buy the NCC group works with the  government on legislation when it comes  to cyber security the owners should  really sit with the manufacturer they're  the people that are designing the toys  they're the people that are responsible  for manufacturing them they need to be  responsible for ensuring that the toy is  safe before it goes on sale whilst the  way these toys are manufactured maybe  the issue here though selling them have  come under intense scrutiny we contacted  all four retailers for a response to the  report but only two responded in a  statement Amazon told Sky News we  require all products offered in our  store to comply with applicable laws and  regulations and we proactively monitor  multiple sources for safety  notifications including from regularity  agencies and direct contacts from brands  manufacturers and sellers whilst John  Lewis said we take the security and  privacy of connected devices very  seriously we have been working to  explore how we can best support the  voluntary code of  practice which improves the security of  connected technology products but now  the consumer group is urging the next  government to bring in specific  legislation to keep children safe we are  calling on the next government to start  looking at mandatory requirements and  manufacturers to ensure that products  are secure before they put them on sale  millions of toys will be wrapped up and  popped under trees across the UK this  Christmas but the drive to get connected  could be coming at the cost of privacy  security and the safety of children in  Zaman rasheed Sky News
 

